1
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:09,566
Deep dive: the export check and the Configure badges. What each finding means, why it is there, and how to clear it properly.

2
00:00:09,566 --> 00:00:22,309
Every export runs the check first. Errors block the export; warnings must be read; info is a reminder. Novak: 0 errors, 16 warnings, 1 info.

3
00:00:22,309 --> 00:00:33,201
PLACEHOLDER_TOOLS: the tool table that ships with the app is invented. Every program would call tools that may not exist on your N-200.

4
00:00:33,201 --> 00:00:48,204
Each tool it uses has a Configure badge: T101 is the cut-out cutter. Number, diameter and depth must match the woodWOP tool database.

5
00:00:48,204 --> 00:00:59,008
MACHINE_PLACEHOLDER: table size, axis travel, tool change, safe Z and spoilboard. The checker uses them to catch parts off the table or cuts too deep.

6
00:00:59,008 --> 00:01:09,368
SMALL_PARTS: the 6" x 2" corner cleat, #41. Vacuum may not hold it once cut free, so the check names the fix: an onion skin, set under Machine & tools.

7
00:01:09,368 --> 00:01:20,414
HORIZONTAL_SKIPPED: this machine profile has no horizontal drill, so edge holes are left out of the program, and listed on the label for drilling by hand.

8
00:01:20,414 --> 00:01:31,202
UNCONFIRMED lists the placeholder values this job actually uses. Fix those first; the rest of the 73 can wait for the jobs that need them.

9
00:01:31,202 --> 00:01:44,799
SIMULATE, the info: generated programs are not machine-proven. The box above is your promise to run each one in woodWOP. It is left unticked here.

10
00:01:44,799 --> 00:01:49,527
The sidebar keeps count: 73 values to configure. Click it to go to Machine & tools.

11
00:01:49,527 --> 00:02:01,809
Configure opens the field for the real value, typed from the tool database. Mark as confirmed keeps the value shown, for when it is already right.

12
00:02:01,809 --> 00:02:11,377
Neither one switches on machine output. That is a separate switch, and it stays off until the shop has simulated real programs.

13
00:02:11,377 --> 00:02:21,290
Import tools reads a CSV export of the real tool table, which clears many badges at once. Faster and safer than typing 73 values.

14
00:02:21,290 --> 00:02:33,706
Badges are not only for the machine. Back in the job: the inside-corner radius of the L parts is a shop fact too.

15
00:02:33,706 --> 00:02:43,689
The edgebander has been checked: it follows a 0.236" (6 mm) inside corner. So the value shown is right: Mark as confirmed.

16
00:02:43,689 --> 00:02:51,847
The badge is gone. A confirmed value stops appearing as unconfirmed in every job and every export check.

17
00:02:51,847 --> 00:03:03,624
Material prices work the same way: a Configure badge until a price is entered (deep dive 11).

18
00:03:03,624 --> 00:03:08,906
So: read the check top to bottom, replace placeholders with real values, confirm only what you have checked, and simulate before you cut.
